Key results
Motter and Lai compared triggers chosen at random with triggers chosen as the node with the largest load, on model networks and on real networks including a power grid and the Internet (Motter and Lai 2002). They found that:

a network with a heterogeneous load distribution, such as a scale-free network, survives a random trigger with little damage but can collapse after the removal of a single high-load node;
a network with a homogeneous load distribution is much less sensitive to the choice of the trigger. Homogeneity of the degrees is not enough: the power grid of the paper has an almost exponential degree distribution and is nevertheless vulnerable to load-based attacks, because its loads are heterogeneous;
\(G\) increases with \(\alpha\), so spare capacity limits the cascade.

The first result parallels the random-failure versus attack asymmetry of Robustness and Percolation, but here one removed node is enough, because the load it carried moves to other nodes.

What a network model can and cannot say
The word “cascade” appears in the official conclusion, and that is the only link to this lab. The cascade on 28 April involved voltage control, reactive power, oscillations, protection settings and the behaviour of generating units (ENTSO-E ICS Investigation Expert Panel 2026). None of these quantities exists in the model you will build.
The Motter-Lai model (Motter and Lai 2002) is an intentionally simplified cascade model:

the grid is a graph with no electrical parameters;
the “load” of a node is the number of shortest paths through it;
a node fails when its load exceeds a fixed capacity;
there is no time, no voltage, no frequency and no control action.

It is not an explanation of the Iberian blackout. It is a minimal model that isolates one mechanism: when a node fails, its load moves to other nodes, and some of those may fail in turn. Use it to ask how network topology and spare capacity change the size of a cascade. Models that include the power flow dynamics exist, for example Schäfer et al. (2018); the Swing Equation page gives a first look.

